# Silent failure is a bug (anti-flake clause 1)

The AIC PIM workspace ships long-running daemons and elevated
configurators. The
[`no-panic.mdc`](no-panic.mdc) rule covers the panic case: every
recoverable failure must propagate via `Result`, not abort. But
"propagate" is not "log" — and the post-2026-05-19 win32
configurator audit caught several call sites where a daemon's
inner task returned `Err(_)` to its caller, the caller forwarded
the error up another level, and three levels later the error
landed in a top-level `select!` arm that just dropped it with a
`Err(_) => {}`. The daemon kept running. The sync silently
stopped working. The configurator showed "last sync: 6 hours
ago" but no failure indication. This is the flake class this
rule targets.

## The rule

> **Every reachable `Err(_)` arm in production code in this
> workspace logs through `tracing` (`warn!` for transient,
> `error!` for definitive) with the operation name, the
> relevant inputs (privacy-appropriate — secrets masked per
> [`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc)
> §5), the error chain (wrapped via `anyhow::Context` or a
> typed `thiserror` enum), and the recovery action taken.
> An early `return Err(e);` without a paired
> `tracing::warn!` / `tracing::error!` at or above the
> originating site is a bug.**

"Caller will log it" is not acceptable. Errors lose context at
each `?` boundary; by the time the error reaches the top of
the call stack, the original op name and inputs are usually
gone. Log at the **originating site** (the place that knows
the op name and inputs) AND let the error propagate. The
top-level handler logs the propagated chain again for
correlation; the two records are not redundant — they answer
different questions ("what failed" vs "how did it unwind").

## Required call-site pattern

```rust
async fn sync_one_cycle(
    http: &HttpClient,
    state: &ServiceState,
) -> Result<SyncOutcome, SyncError> {
    let req = build_sync_request(state)
        .context("build sync request")?;  // logs at site below if it fails
    let resp = match http.execute_with_budget(req, PER_ATTEMPT).await {
        Ok(r) => r,
        Err(HttpError::Timeout { url, elapsed, budget }) => {
            tracing::warn!(
                op = "sync_one_cycle",
                %url,
                ?elapsed,
                ?budget,
                attempt = state.consecutive_failures + 1,
                recovery = "retry next cycle; will pause after MAX_FAILURES",
                "sync HTTP request exceeded per-attempt budget",
            );
            return Err(SyncError::Transport(format!("timeout after {:?}", elapsed)));
        }
        Err(HttpError::Transport { url, source, elapsed }) => {
            tracing::warn!(
                op = "sync_one_cycle",
                %url,
                ?elapsed,
                error = format!("{:#}", anyhow::Error::new(source)),
                attempt = state.consecutive_failures + 1,
                recovery = "retry next cycle",
                "sync HTTP transport error",
            );
            return Err(SyncError::Transport("transport failure".into()));
        }
        Err(other) => {
            tracing::error!(
                op = "sync_one_cycle",
                error = ?other,
                recovery = "abort cycle; mark unhealthy in runtime_status.json",
                "sync HTTP request failed with unhandled error variant",
            );
            return Err(SyncError::Unhandled(other.to_string()));
        }
    };
    handle_response(resp).await
}
```

Note the recovery action is named in every log record. The
operator reading the rolling log can answer "is the daemon
trying again?" without source access.

## Forbidden constructs

- `Err(_) => {}` / `let _ = future.await;` on a non-trivial
 future. The wildcard / discard is the silent-failure shape
 the rule targets. Match on the specific error variant and
 log.
- `return Err(e);` without a `tracing::warn!` / `tracing::error!`
 at the same site (and outside `#[cfg(test)]`). The single
 exception is when the function name itself is the op name
 and the immediate caller has its own log site that knows
 the inputs.
- `Result<_, _>` returned from a top-level `tokio::spawn` task
 body without an outer wrapper that catches the result and
 logs on `Err`. A spawned task whose `JoinHandle` is dropped
 swallows panics AND `Err` returns silently.
- `tracing::debug!` / `tracing::trace!` as the only log on a
 reachable error path. Errors are not debug noise; they are
 either `warn!` (transient — retry will likely succeed) or
 `error!` (definitive — manual intervention needed).
- A `tracing::error!` followed by `Ok(())` "to avoid breaking
 the loop". If the error was definitive enough to log at
 `error!`, the loop must reflect the failure in its return
 type, its state machine, or its `runtime_status.json`
 fingerprint (R7) — not silently continue.
- `.map_err(|_| MyError::Something)` discarding the source
 error. Always preserve the source: `.map_err(|e|
 MyError::Something { source: e.into() })` or
 `.context("doing X")?`.

## Required log shape

Every error log record uses structured fields, not message
interpolation:

```rust
tracing::warn!(
    op = "snake_case_op_name",
    target_kind = "tenant" /* or "agent", "endpoint", "settings_file" */,
    target = %target_name_or_id_or_url_redacted,
    error = format!("{:#}", err),     // full anyhow chain
    recovery = "retry|skip|pause|abort_and_unhealth",
    "human-readable summary in US English",
);
```

This shape is searchable in Loki / Splunk / Datadog and
correlates with the Windows Event Log entries (which use the
same field names per
[`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §5).

## Silent empty states in the admin SPA

The same defect class has a front-end shape, and it is the more
expensive one because the operator, not a log reader, is the
person misled.

> **A page whose data request failed must not render as an empty
> shell. A failed query renders a visible, readable error state
> that says the request failed, and it must be distinguishable at
> a glance from "loaded successfully, no rows yet."**

Three states, three distinct renderings, never collapsed into
one:

| State | Render |
| --- | --- |
| **Loading** | Skeleton or spinner |
| **Loaded, empty** | Neutral empty state — "No scan runs yet." Plus the action that creates the first one |
| **Failed** | `Alert severity="error"` naming what failed, the HTTP status when there is one, and a Retry control |

Requirements at every data-fetching page or panel:

1. **Consume the error channel.** With TanStack Query, branch on
   `isError` / `error`, not only on `isLoading` and `data.length`.
   A hook whose `error` is never read is this bug.
2. **Fail loudly in the console too.** Log the failing method,
   the resolved URL, and the status, so the browser devtools
   answer "why is this empty?" without a debugger.
3. **The error text is readable**, per
   [`readable-read-only-controls.mdc`](readable-read-only-controls.mdc)
   and Section 508 — normal contrast, `role="alert"`, not a
   faded caption.
4. **Never swallow.** `.catch(() => {})`, `.catch(() => [])`, and
   `try { … } catch {}` around a fetch are forbidden; an empty
   array returned from a `catch` is indistinguishable from real
   emptiness and is exactly how two days were lost.
5. **`data ?? []` is not error handling.** It renders "no data"
   for a 404, a 500, and a network partition alike.

**Worked violation to fix** — the netscan and
known-default-credentials pages, August 2026. Every request 404'd
on a doubled `/api/api/...` path (see
[`api-client-path-composition.mdc`](../../pim-offline-server/.cursor/rules/api-client-path-composition.mdc)),
the components rendered `rows={data ?? []}`, and the operator saw
tidy empty grids. Nothing anywhere said "request failed."

```tsx
// Violation
const { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['netscan'], queryFn: fetchRuns })
return <DataGrid rows={data ?? []} />

// Fix
const { data, isLoading, isError, error, refetch } = useQuery(...)
if (isLoading) return <Skeleton />
if (isError)
  return (
    <Alert severity="error" role="alert"
           action={<Button onClick={() => refetch()}>Retry</Button>}>
      Could not load scan runs: {describeApiError(error)}
    </Alert>
  )
if (data.length === 0) return <EmptyState message="No scan runs yet." />
return <DataGrid rows={data} />
```

An empty grid then means empty, and a broken page announces
itself the first time it is opened.

## Worked violation → fix — the scheduler's HTTP loop

**Violation** (today, conceptual, in
`pim-offline-client/src/service_scheduler/`):

```rust
loop {
    tokio::select! {
        _ = shutdown.changed() => break,
        _ = ticker.tick() => {
            if let Err(_) = sync_one_cycle(&http, &state).await {
                state.consecutive_failures += 1;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

`sync_one_cycle` returns a rich error variant; the loop discards
it. The configurator shows `consecutive_failures = 47` six
hours later and the operator has no idea why. The Windows Event
Log has nothing. Loki shows nothing. The log file shows nothing
above `debug` level.

**Fix**:

```rust
loop {
    tokio::select! {
        biased;
        _ = shutdown.changed() => {
            tracing::info!(op = "sync_loop", "yielding to shutdown");
            break;
        }
        _ = ticker.tick() => {
            match sync_one_cycle(&http, &state).await {
                Ok(outcome) => {
                    tracing::info!(
                        op = "sync_loop",
                        records = outcome.records,
                        elapsed_ms = outcome.elapsed.as_millis() as u64,
                        "sync cycle complete",
                    );
                    state.consecutive_failures = 0;
                    state.last_success = Some(OffsetDateTime::now_utc());
                }
                Err(SyncError::Transport(msg)) => {
                    state.consecutive_failures += 1;
                    tracing::warn!(
                        op = "sync_loop",
                        consecutive_failures = state.consecutive_failures,
                        error = %msg,
                        recovery = if state.consecutive_failures < MAX_FAILURES {
                            "retry on next tick"
                        } else {
                            "pausing scheduler; mark unhealthy"
                        },
                        "sync cycle failed (transport)",
                    );
                    if state.consecutive_failures >= MAX_FAILURES {
                        runtime_status::mark_unhealthy("sync", &msg);
                        scheduler::pause(&shutdown).await;
                    }
                }
                Err(SyncError::Unhandled(msg)) => {
                    state.consecutive_failures += 1;
                    tracing::error!(
                        op = "sync_loop",
                        error = %msg,
                        recovery = "pause scheduler; require operator intervention",
                        "sync cycle failed with unhandled error",
                    );
                    runtime_status::mark_unhealthy("sync", &msg);
                    scheduler::pause(&shutdown).await;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Every error path logs. The operator's "last sync 6 hours ago"
question is now answerable from the rolling log alone, no
debugger required. The configurator's runtime_status badge
(R7) AND the Windows Event Log (R9) reflect the unhealthy
state.

## Where this is currently enforced

| Surface | Status |
| --- | --- |
| `pim-offline-client` service scheduler | Wave 1 Track D adds the structured error-log shape |
| `pim-offline-client` HTTP client error handling | Wave 1 Track D |
| `pim-offline-server` admin API handlers | partial — already structured at the route handler level; audit ongoing for inner helpers |
| `pim-offline-server` background jobs (audit log writer, OTLP exporter) | audit ongoing |
| All four configurator GUIs | inherits library logging shape via `pim-app-config-cli::dialog_helpers` |
| `pim-app-config` CLI (`set` / `unset` / `get` / `orphans`) | inherits via R2 exit codes |
| Admin SPA data-fetching pages (`pim-offline-server/ui`) | audit ongoing — netscan and known-default-credentials rendered empty shells on 404 (2026-08-15) |

